# goal The surface that actually fits ACP

The Responses API is stateful and so is ACP; chat completions is not, and pays for
it. Everything here follows from that one alignment — see
[sessions.js.hint#conversation_unit](./sessions.js.hint) for the retention side.

# invariant Pure translation, like openai.js {#pure}

No I/O, no clock beyond what callers pass in. Response ids come from `sessions.js`
and timestamps from the caller, so a response object is reproducible in a test.

# rule A continued turn sends only the new input {#no_replay}

This is the whole advantage over chat completions and the easiest thing to undo by
accident. The session already holds the history; resending it would make the agent
read its own past a second time, as text, alongside its real memory of it.

So `toInputBlocks` renders **only** what arrived in this request. Any change that
reconstructs a transcript from stored responses is wrong.

This is also why a suspended tool turn cannot use chat's discard-and-replay escape
hatch. Chat receives the whole history again; Responses receives only the new
input. While the tool turn is pending, new input without a matching
`function_call_output` therefore gets 409 `tool_result_expected` with instructions
to provide the result or omit `previous_response_id`. Once the turn settles
unattended and releases itself, later input continues the same retained session and
keeps its context.

# rule reasoning.effort is per request, and re-applied every turn {#per_request_effort}

The one genuinely per-request agent setting ACP can carry, via the `thought_level`
config option. It is applied through `Agent.turn`'s `overrides` on every turn,
including on a session that already exists — a caller may raise it for one hard
question in the middle of a conversation and drop it again after.

Responses uses `reasoning.effort`; Chat uses its separate `reasoning_effort` field.
Both map to the same ACP category, but their request spellings must not leak across
the two public APIs.

# func parseResponsesRequest {#parse}

## result
`{model, blocks, inputBlocks, instructions, previousResponseId, store, stream,
reasoning, maxTokens, stop, ignored, tools, toolChoice, toolsProvided,
toolResults}`. `blocks` includes the instructions preamble for a new chain;
`inputBlocks` contains only current input and is mandatory for continuations so
unchanged instructions are not duplicated.

## rule store defaults to true
OpenAI stores by default, and without retention `previous_response_id` could never
be satisfied. `store: false` closes the session with the turn. When caller tools
are actively served, the server rejects `store: false` with 400 `store_required`:
the agent may suspend inside a call, and retaining the response id is the only
honest way for a later `function_call_output` request to reach that turn.

## rule unsupported_parameter errors
`text` (structured output), `include`, `truncation`, `tool_choice: required` or a
named choice, non-function tools, and `strict: true` — the same style/contract
split as chat completions, under this API's names — throw `RequestError` with HTTP
400 and code `unsupported_parameter`. `tool_choice: auto` is served; `none`
disables caller tools for the turn. See [_.hint#param_policy](../_.hint).

On a continuation, declaring tools is accepted only when the conversation already
owns a tool bench. Adding tools to an unbenched retained session is 400: MCP servers
are fixed at `session/new`, and silently reopening would lose Responses context.

# func responseObject {#shape}

## rule status comes from the ACP stop reason, and incompleteness is stated
`max_tokens` becomes `status: "incomplete"` with
`incomplete_details.reason: "max_output_tokens"`, never a `completed` response
holding a truncated answer. `refusal` becomes incomplete/content_filter and
`cancelled` uses the official cancelled status. When no exact OpenAI mapping exists,
the valid nearest enum is used and `x_acp2api.stop_reason` preserves the ACP value.
Reasoning becomes its own `reasoning` output item ahead of the message, matching
where OpenAI puts it.

The response object carries the same append-only `x_acp2api` facts as Chat. Context
and cost remain absent when the agent did not report them. In a stream the object
rides the existing terminal `response.completed` event; no new event type is
created. Retirement replay attribution is Chat-only: a retired
`previous_response_id` is already visible as 404 and must not be converted into a
fresh Responses conversation.

# rule The stream is ordered, and that ordering is the contract {#event_order}

Unlike chat completions' uniform chunks, these events are typed and sequenced: an
item is added before its parts and closed after them, `output_index` increases, and
`sequence_number` is dense so a consumer can detect a gap. A stray delta for an item
that was never opened breaks a compliant client.

`ResponseStream` owns that sequencing precisely so it cannot be violated by writes
scattered through a request handler. Switching between reasoning and message text
closes the open item first — the two are separate output items, not one interleaved
stream.

`finalize(response)` closes the open item and returns a new canonical response
snapshot. The caller must finalize before response-store admission and byte
accounting. Terminal emitters never mutate that snapshot or their input object.

Each data frame also carries `event: <payload.type>`; the terminal `[DONE]` sentinel
remains data-only. A post-header failure uses `event: error` with the standard error
envelope. Chat completions deliberately remains data-only.

# invariant Responses only renames canonical usage

`renameUsage` consumes [openai.js.hint#canonical_usage](./openai.js.hint) and changes
field names only. Cache arithmetic and totals must never be reimplemented here.

# decision Responses conversations are linear, not snapshots

Only the current tip may be continued. After A -> B, continuing A is 409
`stale_previous_response`; an unknown or irrecoverable id is 404 `not_found`.
Arbitrary branching cannot alias an old id to the current ACP session because that
session already includes later turns.

Instructions are a text preamble, not an ACP system role. An identical explicit
value may continue without being sent a second time; changing, adding, or removing
it is 400 `unsupported_parameter`. The protocol reference is
https://developers.openai.com/api/reference/cli/resources/responses/methods/create.

# rule Responses stream items have one canonical lifecycle

Initial `response.created` and `response.in_progress` objects have status
`in_progress` and no output items. Reasoning summary events use `summary_index` and
`response.reasoning_summary_part.added/done`, unlike message content events.
Interleaved message/reasoning/message runs need distinct item identities and a
terminal output array consistent with emitted indexes. Initial response events
must describe an in-progress response. Function calls always emit item added,
arguments delta/done, and item done even when ACP supplies one complete argument
string. These rules follow
https://developers.openai.com/api/reference/resources/responses/streaming-events
and must also be exercised through an official SDK accumulator; a local SSE parser
alone is not compatibility evidence.

# rule Responses input is validated before ACP rendering

`input_text` and returned `output_text` become text. `input_image` accepts only a
base64 `data:` URI and becomes an ACP image. `input_file.file_data` accepts the same
inline data URI contract as Chat and becomes an ACP embedded resource. Remote URLs
and `file_id` are rejected, not fetched or dropped. Unsupported reasoning,
item-reference, encrypted, and computer items are explicit 400 errors. Roles and
nested text fields must have their declared string types; malformed content never
becomes an empty prompt. Unsupported keys within `reasoning` are reported as dotted
paths in `x_acp2api.ignored`, never silently discarded.

# test responses

- `input` accepts a bare string, `{role, content}` messages, `input_text`, returned
  `output_text`, data-URI `input_image`, and inline `input_file.file_data` parts
- inline files require the agent's ACP `embeddedContext` capability; `file_id` and
  remote `file_url` are rejected
- `instructions` become an unlabelled system preamble
- unsupported input item types are rejected rather than silently dropped
- `previous_response_id`, `reasoning.effort`, `max_output_tokens`, `store` are mapped
- `tools` and trailing `function_call_output` items are mapped; `text` is 400;
  `temperature` is ignored and reported
- stop reasons map only to official status/detail enums; lossy mappings preserve
  the ACP reason in `x_acp2api.stop_reason`
- reasoning appears as its own output item before the message
- context and cost annotations are present only when reported, including on the terminal streamed response object
- a conversation continues in the **same** ACP session, provable by its session id
- only the latest response in a chain continues; an older known id is 409 stale
- an unknown `previous_response_id` is 404; a mismatched model is 400
- `store: false` answers and retains nothing
- `store: false` with served tools is 400 `store_required`
- tools on an unbenched continuation are 400 without damaging the stored chain
- new input with no tool outputs gets 409 while the tool response is pending; after
  unattended settlement releases it, new input continues the same ACP session
- event lines match payload types; the stream is densely sequenced and correctly
  nested; reasoning indexes, interleaved item IDs, function-call events, and the
  terminal output array agree
- stream finalization precedes response byte accounting and mutates no admitted object
